---
import { MARKET_DATASETS } from '@glideworth/core';
import ToolPage from '../../components/tools/ToolPage.astro';
import { depletionYears } from '../../tools/closed-form';
import { engineYears, HISTORY_WINDOW, historyYears } from '../../tools/engine/how-long';
import { HISTORY_DATASET } from '../../tools/engine/model';
import { money, wholeYears } from '../../tools/format';

const m = (x: number) => money(x, 'USD');
const ds = MARKET_DATASETS[HISTORY_DATASET];
// The article's worked example, computed by the calculator's own engine code at build time.
const ex = { balance: 500_000, spend: 30_000, ret: 4, age: 65, tax: 0, stocks: 60, timing: 'end', cur: 'USD' } as const;
const end = engineYears(ex);
const start = engineYears({ ...ex, timing: 'start' });
const byReturn = [2, 3, 4, 5, 6].map((r) => {
  const e = engineYears({ ...ex, ret: r });
  const f = depletionYears(500_000, 30_000, r / 100, 'end');
  return [`${r}%`, Number.isFinite(f) ? f.toFixed(1) : 'never', Number.isFinite(e.years) ? e.years.toFixed(1) : 'never'];
});
const hist = historyYears(ex);
const lasted = hist.filter((h) => h.full).length;
const worst = hist.reduce((a, b) => (b.years < a.years ? b : a));

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How do I work out how long my money will last?',
    a: '<p>With a fixed yearly withdrawal <em>W</em> from a balance <em>B</em> earning a steady real return <em>r</em>, taken at the end of each year, the money lasts −ln(1 − r × B ÷ W) ÷ ln(1 + r) years. 500,000 with 30,000 a year at 4% lasts 28.0 years. This calculator runs the same plan through the planning engine and replays it through US market history.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should the return be real or nominal?',
    a: '<p>Real (after inflation), because the withdrawal is in today’s money and rises with inflation. Using a nominal return with a real withdrawal overstates how long the money lasts.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does history give such different answers from the formula?',
    a: '<p>The formula assumes the same return every year. Real returns vary, and when you are withdrawing, the order matters: bad years early do more damage than the same years late. That is sequence-of-returns risk, and the history chart shows its range.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I include a pension?',
    a: '<p>Only roughly here, by lowering the withdrawal by the pension. If income starts or stops part-way, the full planner or the Monte Carlo calculator’s income option handles it year by year.</p>',
  },
];
---

<ToolPage
  id="how-long"
  heading="How long will my money last?"
  lede="How many years your savings pay a yearly withdrawal at a steady return, then the same plan replayed through every start year of US market history since 1928. It runs the Glideworth planning engine in your browser; nothing you type is sent anywhere."
  faq={faq}
  adds="This calculator has one withdrawal, one return and no other income."
  related={[
    { href: '/blog/monte-carlo-retirement-simulations/', label: 'Monte Carlo retirement simulations: reading the result' },
    { href: '/features/monte-carlo/', label: 'Monte Carlo and historical backtests in the planner' },
    { href: '/docs/methodology/historical-data/', label: 'The historical data the engine uses' },
  ]}
>
  <h2>How the calculator works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Steady return.</strong> The engine projects your savings year by year at your return after inflation,
      taking the withdrawal each year (rising with inflation, so it stays the same in today’s money). The answer is the
      number of years paid in full plus the share of the next year it could still pay.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>History.</strong> The engine then replays the same withdrawal through real US returns and inflation,
      starting once in every year from {ds.firstYear} that leaves a full {HISTORY_WINDOW}-year window, with a mix of
      stocks and 10-year Treasury bonds (60/40 by default), rebalanced yearly. The chart shows how many years each start
      year paid in full, up to {HISTORY_WINDOW}.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The formula</h2>
  <p class="formula">years = −ln(1 − r × B ÷ W) ÷ ln(1 + r)</p>
  <p>
    <code>B</code> is the starting balance, <code>W</code> the yearly withdrawal taken at the end of each year and
    <code>r</code> the real return. It only has an answer when r × B is less than W; otherwise growth covers the
    withdrawal and the money never runs out. For withdrawals at the start of the year, replace W with W × (1 + r); for
    the middle of the year, W × (1 + r)<sup>½</sup>. With a flat tax on withdrawals, W is the amount you spend divided by
    (1 − tax).
  </p>

  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    Computed by the calculator’s own code when this page was built. {m(500_000)}, withdrawals of {m(30_000)} at the end
    of each year, 4% after inflation: r × B = {m(20_000)}, 1 − 20,000 ÷ 30,000 = 0.33333, −ln(0.33333) = 1.09861 and
    ln(1.04) = 0.03922, so the formula gives {depletionYears(500_000, 30_000, 0.04, 'end').toFixed(2)} years. The
    engine pays {Math.floor(end.years)} full years: after 27 withdrawals {m(end.path[27] ?? 0)} is left, after 28 only
    {' '}{m(end.path[28] ?? 0)}, so its answer is {end.years.toFixed(2)} years. With withdrawals at the start of each
    year instead, it is {start.years.toFixed(1)} years.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Same balance and withdrawal at other steady returns (end-of-year withdrawals)</caption>
    <thead><tr><th>Return after inflation</th><th>Formula (years)</th><th>Engine (years)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{byReturn.map((r) => <tr>{r.map((c) => <td>{c}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Through history, the same {m(500_000)} paying {m(30_000)} a year with 60% in stocks lasted the whole
    {' '}{HISTORY_WINDOW}-year window in {lasted} of {hist.length} start years from {hist[0]?.startYear} to
    {' '}{hist[hist.length - 1]?.startYear}. The shortest, starting in {worst.startYear}, paid {wholeYears(worst.years)}
    {' '}in full: a steady-return answer of 28 years hides that range.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the engine and the formula can differ</h2>
  <p>
    With a positive return they agree to a hundredth of a year. The engine credits each withdrawal with the share of
    the year’s return set by the timing option, exactly as the formula’s W′ does, and the last withdrawal can only take
    what is left at the moment it is made. With a negative return and start or middle timing the engine can differ by a
    few hundredths of a year, because in a losing year it does not let a withdrawal exceed the reduced balance.
  </p>

  <h2>What this assumes and leaves out</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A steady return in the first answer. Real returns vary, and their order matters while you withdraw.</li>
    <li>A fixed withdrawal in today’s money: no cuts in bad years, no pension or other income.</li>
    <li>No fees, and no tax unless you set the flat rate.</li>
    <li>
      History is one country’s past ({ds.label}): a record of what happened there, not a forecast. Data: Aswath
      Damodaran, <em>Historical Returns on Stocks, Bonds and Bills</em> (NYU Stern), and US Bureau of Labor Statistics
      CPI-U. Start years are not independent: consecutive windows share most of their years.
    </li>
  </ul>
</ToolPage>
